  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nAt 11 AM, temperatures were running 2 to 8 degrees cooler than \nthey were at that time yesterday across the area. A gradual \ncooling is expected each day through Tuesday, with minor warming \nexpected for the latter half of the week. Even with the warming, \ntemperatures are expected to stay near to slightly above average \nfor this time of year. \n\nBroad troughing is expected prevail over the western United States \nthrough at least the middle of the week, which will keep the flow \naloft southwesterly and cut down the humidity. In addition, winds \nwill increase each afternoon with the sea breeze, most noticeable \nfor mountains, passes, and canyons. Latest forecast has backed off \non precipitation from Tropical Cyclone Karina, with current chances \nnow less than 15 percent. The most likely scenario is there will be \nmore widespread high cloud coverage Tuesday into Wednesday. The \nmarine layer is expected to continue to rebuild over the next few \ndays, with the potential for night and morning low clouds to reach \ninto the western valleys. Depending on the extent of high cloud \ncoverage mid-week, low cloud coverage may be disrupted again. \n\nLate in the week, ensembles begin to diverge on the placement of the \nupper trough near the West Coast and the upper high over the \nSouthern Rockies, with a trend towards a more eastward placement of \neach. This more eastward placement of the ridge and troughing closer \nto the coast would limit the amount of warming late in the week into \nnext weekend. This would also affect the steering flow of a \npotential tropical system and any moisture and precipitation we'd \nreceive from it over the weekend into early next week. While the \nNational Hurricane Center has a 90% chance of this disturbance \ndeveloping into a tropical cyclone off the coast of Mexico in the \nnext 7 days, it is far too early to tell what, if any, impacts we'll \nsee locally, especially given the ensemble spread in the upper level \npattern. Current forecast has at least a chance of precipitation\nnext weekend.",
